n: 3.1
name: nums-core
kind: hub
is: The nums package of NUMS.Go (7,045 lines of Go in 15 files, last changed 2023-03-17) represents a subset of Go as nodes with a four-field ID Package.Level.Type.Instance: a fixed catalogue at levels 1 and 2, composites minted by arrival order from level 3. Names and literal text sit in side tables; a full rename changed no node. The package executes and stores nothing, does not build as checked in, builds with four lines removed, and panics on its three shipped samples. Numbering varies between runs. A cell is only a declared named slot.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1
name: standing
kind: hub
is: The nums package is a 2023 prototype of 7,045 lines that represents a subset of Go as numbers and executes nothing. It does not build as checked in, builds with four lines removed, completes on small files and panics on its three shipped samples; the request's description of it as fully working does not hold.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.2
name: nature
kind: observation
is: The nums package is 7,045 lines of Go in 15 files, 1,502 of them in go.go, with no test file. It is a static representation of Go source in a numeric space plus a partial printer back to Go text. It holds no evaluator, interpreter or code generator, and no call that writes a file: both node databases live in memory, per module.
status: observed
src: 2.4.13#6-17
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.3
name: build
kind: observation
is: As checked in, go build and go vet in nums/ exit 1 under go1.27.2: line 8 of go.work and line 5 of nums/go.mod point at a sibling directory, <tree-sitter-fork>, that is absent. In a copy with four lines removed (that replace line, and the verilog import and case in parser.go) both commands exit 0 offline against the pinned upstream binding.
status: observed
src: 2.4.18#8 2.4.17#5 2.4.6#24 2.4.6#148-149
limit: One machine, darwin/arm64. The build needs cgo and a C compiler. The absent directory is a private fork that added a Verilog grammar; whether the fork changed anything else is unknown.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.4
name: worked-example
kind: finding
is: For a 14-line Go file the reading's dump shows 3 blueprint and 14 recipe entries. The expression a + b is recipe 1.3.0.2, stored as 0.2.3.1+0.1.11.1+0.1.11.2 (Math +, Parameter 1, Parameter 2). Function add is two entries: a signature blueprint 1.3.0.2 (Function of return and parameter types) and recipe 1.3.0.4, stored as 0.2.18.1+1.3.0.1+1.6.0.1.
status: assumed
limit: The numbers come from one run of a dump program in a session scratch directory that was not kept; member ordinals in the same dump vary between runs.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.5
name: samples-panic
kind: observation
is: Built from the copy, nums completes on the reading's 14-line example and 17 single-feature probes and panics with a nil dereference on all three Go samples shipped in the repository. On sample/sample.go, which the repository's 19-line driver loads, 36 functions and 547 nodes are recorded and 68 ASSERT lines logged first; assertion helpers only log.
status: observed
src: 2.4.20#13 2.4.16#5-18 2.4.7#1010
limit: Whether the three samples completed at an earlier commit was not checked.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.6
name: probe-matrix
kind: observation
is: In the reading's probes of nums, arithmetic, comparison, if/else, return, assignment, literals, pointers, struct fields and calls are recorded and print back as Go. Three-clause for, range, closures, composite literals, and go or defer on a later-declared callee are recorded but do not print back; map indexing and switch are not recorded.
status: observed
src: 2.4.7#916-922 2.4.12#147-153
limit: No probe of the reading holds &&, || or !; a reviewer probe (if !a && b || a), run again for this node, is recorded and prints back unchanged. Range is recorded as 'Access range' over auto-declared globals, not as a Loop node; go and defer on a later-declared callee carry the no-node ID 0.0.0.0 as callee.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.1.8
name: premise-against-runs
kind: finding
is: The request describes NUMS.Go as a fully working reference. For the nums package at commit 9e6bbb4 the code and the runs show otherwise: the idea is demonstrated for a core subset of Go, and the implementation is a 2023 prototype that executes nothing, does not build as checked in and crashes on its own samples.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.1.2 3.1.1.3 3.1.1.5 3.1.1.6
limit: A C++ NUMS that the Go code was translated from was not found; if it exists and executes, the description may hold there.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2
name: space-and-store
kind: hub
is: A NUMS ID is four fields, Package.Level.Type.Instance, in 80 bits; levels 1 and 2 are a fixed catalogue from many languages and levels 3 to 21 are composites. A node is a category plus ordered child IDs, minted by arrival order with a use count; declarations change content under a fixed ID, and a cell is a declared named slot, not an instance.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.1
name: node-id
kind: observation
is: NodeID_t has four fields, Package.Level.Type.Instance, of 16, 16, 16 and 32 bits (80 bits), written P.L.T.I. Two ID spaces reuse the struct: level 1 type 2 is Bool in the recipe space and Numeric in the blueprint space, and only the Go type, RecipeNodeID_t or BlueprintNodeID_t, says which space a number belongs to.
status: observed
src: 2.4.2#11-24 2.4.2#55-57 2.4.2#91 2.4.2#640 2.4.3#60 2.4.3#108
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.2
name: levels
kind: observation
is: In NUMS, level 0 is undefined, level 1 holds leaves, level 2 a fixed catalogue of operators and structural kinds, and levels 3 to 21 composites minted at run time. A composite's level is one more than the largest of its category level and its children's levels; reserved nodes (functions, structs, interfaces, packages) stay at level 3.
status: observed
src: 2.4.3#5-29 2.4.2#814-830 2.4.4#168-170
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.3
name: recipe-catalogue
kind: observation
is: The NUMS recipe catalogue has 18 leaf types at level 1 (Null to Exception) and 19 operator types at level 2 (Compare to TypeDecl). Its instances include <=>, ===, >>> and malloc, which are not Go, and its comments cite C, C# and Python forms. A plain assignment to a local is recorded as Write and one through a pointer parameter as TempWrite.
status: observed
src: 2.4.3#56-80 2.4.3#125-150 2.4.3#215-216 2.4.3#280 2.4.3#375 2.4.9#49-60 2.4.9#84-86 2.4.9#392-413 2.4.9#516-553 2.4.7#627-630 2.4.7#655
limit: The catalogue comment defines TempWrite as a write with no global state change. An assignment statement gets it only when its left side is an Access node holding no global or member; the write lowered from := and the field writes of a composite literal also get it. Run on a scratch copy with four lines removed.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.4
name: blueprint-catalogue-and-edges
kind: observation
is: NUMS blueprint leaves are Void and Numeric (Bool, Integer, Decimal, Imag, Complex, Char, String); level 2 has Reference, Container, Recipe and Data. A blueprint's child is an ID plus a 23-bit attribute mask written ID^mask, so public, embedded or parent is a property of the reference. The code sets 8 of the 23 bits. Recipe children are bare IDs.
status: observed
src: 2.4.3#104-113 2.4.3#175-182 2.4.3#534-548 2.4.3#781-807 2.4.2#743-764 2.4.11#23-35 2.4.8#81-102
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.5
name: composite-node
kind: observation
is: A NUMS composite node is a category ID from the catalogue plus an ordered list of child IDs, serialized as category+child+child for recipes and category+child^edge+child^edge for blueprints. A node holds no name, no source position, no literal value and no comment.
status: observed
src: 2.4.2#727-731 2.4.2#780-785 2.4.2#866-886
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.6
name: minting-by-ordinal
kind: observation
is: Record_Entry in NUMS stores a node under its serialized string. A string already present returns the existing ID and adds to a use count; a new one gets instance len(Entries)+1 at its level, package 1, and type 0 from every caller. A minted ID is therefore 1.<level>.0.<arrival ordinal>, local to one in-memory module; catalogue IDs carry package 0.
status: observed
src: 2.4.4#55-80 2.4.2#16 2.4.2#735 2.4.2#789 2.4.2#290-294
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.7
name: reserved-nodes-mutate
kind: observation
is: A NUMS node whose string has no child is stored as category=key, so it is never found by value and each such declaration gets its own ID; Update_Entry later overwrites that entry in place. Structs, interfaces, packages and function declarations are therefore nominal and change content under a fixed ID, while other nodes are structural.
status: observed
src: 2.4.4#69-72 2.4.4#82-85 2.4.10#265-281
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.2.8
name: named-cell
kind: observation
is: NamedCell_t in NUMS holds an access recipe, a base blueprint, a name and a constructor recipe, and the blueprint handle returns fields and members as that struct. The two-field Cell_t is unused. NUMS therefore represents a cell as a declared named slot; a cell has no run-time instance and no value there.
status: observed
src: 2.4.12#497-502 2.4.14#73 2.4.14#93 2.4.14#172-184 2.4.15#3-6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3
name: names-and-lens
kind: hub
is: Names and literal text sit in side tables of small ordinals: a full rename changes no node, while numbering still varies between runs through Go map order. Text enters through tree-sitter and 65 Go-only handlers that declare whatever does not resolve, and leaves through a printer that covers a subset of function bodies and is called only by a log.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.1
name: symbol-tables
kind: observation
is: NUMS keeps names in two tables outside the node databases: one per recipe (locals, parameters, integers, decimals, strings, literals, labels, each text to ordinal) and one per module (blueprint, member, global and function names to IDs). An ordinal is len(map)+1 on first sight; going from number to name is a linear scan of a Go map.
status: observed
src: 2.4.5#9-15 2.4.5#85-103 2.4.8#209-234
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.2
name: rename-experiment
kind: finding
is: Replacing every identifier of the reading's 14-line Go example with a different word changed no NUMS node: over 20 runs each, the blueprint and recipe databases of the original and the renamed file fell into the same two byte-identical dumps.
status: assumed
limit: One example file; the dump program lived in a session scratch directory that was not kept.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.3
name: literals-outside-tree
kind: observation
is: NUMS stores literal values as source text in the per-recipe table, not in the tree. In the reading's probe, a function returning a + 1 and one returning b + 2 produced the identical body node (1.5.0.1, use count 2); their tables were Integers {"1": 1} and Integers {"2": 1}. A recipe's meaning is its tree plus its table.
status: observed
src: 2.4.5#11
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.4
name: numbering-not-deterministic
kind: observation
is: NUMS numbering differs between runs of the same input. Member ordinals are handed out while ranging over a Go map in Fetch_Fields, which is reached each time a blueprint handle is made in order to fill a display string. Over 20 runs the two numberings split 19 to 1 for the reading's example file and 16 to 4 for its renamed copy.
status: observed
src: 2.4.14#14-26 2.4.14#185-187
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.5
name: parser-front
kind: observation
is: NUMS delegates parsing to tree-sitter through a cgo binding. Thirteen languages are enumerated; only Go has handlers, any other returns a not-supported error. Go has 75 dispatch entries for 65 handlers and three generic functions, keyed by 75 of 213 hand-copied symbol numbers, all matching the pinned grammar; 62 carry their rule as a comment.
status: observed
src: 2.4.6#11-24 2.4.6#29-47 2.4.6#451-461 2.4.6#631-657 2.4.7#1209-1502 2.4.7#185 2.4.7#1197-1207 2.4.22#golang/parser.c
limit: The 213 names and numbers were compared with the upstream grammar at the pinned version, not with the private fork the repository points at. true_literal, false_literal and nil_literal have no comment; int_literal serves two symbols, Token three and Sequence five.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.6
name: auto-declare-and-lossy
kind: observation
is: In NUMS a name that resolves as no local, parameter, member, function, global or blueprint is declared at once: a local in a variable declaration, a member after a package or struct, a function when called, else a global. Go's integer types except uintptr share one Integer blueprint, both floats one Decimal; struct tags and comments are dropped.
status: observed
src: 2.4.9#198-304 2.4.7#41-53 2.4.7#418 2.4.7#1300 2.4.6#623-625 2.4.6#640-642
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.3.7
name: printer
kind: observation
is: The NUMS writer prints Go-like text for compare, logic, math, bit-math, assignment, member access, subscript, blocks, if/else, cast, return, go, defer and call. Loops, function declarations, new and make, switch and labelled jumps are not handled, and no code prints a function header or a file. Its only call site in the package is a log line.
status: observed
src: 2.4.12#88-351 2.4.8#370
limit: What comes back is a normalized function body, not the source text: c := a + b printed as var c int followed by c = a + b.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.4
name: needs
kind: hub
is: What a shared, executable numeric root needs that NUMS lacks: identity computed from canonical content, with no mutation under an ID; a meaning for each catalogue entry, blueprints precise enough to emit code, cell instances and a decision on literals; and lenses held as tables keyed by catalogue ID with a reader small enough for a bootstrap.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.4.2
name: needs-meaning
kind: finding
is: NUMS has a vocabulary and a tree and no rule for what a catalogue entry does. Each entry needs a meaning the bootstrap can run, blueprints need width, signedness and layout (one Integer and one Decimal cannot drive machine code), cells need instances with values, and the design must choose whether literal values enter a recipe's identity.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.1.2 3.1.2.8 3.1.3.3 3.1.3.6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.4.3
name: needs-lenses-as-data
kind: finding
is: NUMS reads Go through about 1,500 lines of handlers bound to one grammar version's symbol numbers and a cgo parser, and writes through a hard-coded switch with Go spellings. Many languages need both directions as tables keyed by catalogue ID and a reader small enough for the bootstrap; the {0} {1} templates in the constant names are a first form.
status: inferred
conf: mid
src: 2.4.3#212 2.4.3#300 2.4.2#516-535
from: 3.1.3.5 3.1.3.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: Seven things to carry forward from NUMS: the four-part address in three tiers; a node as category plus ordered child IDs, stored once with a use count; names and literals as a detachable table; attributes on the reference and a function as signature plus body; a cross-language catalogue that names effect; the rule beside its handler; a no-node ID.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8.1
name: four-part-address-three-tiers
kind: finding
is: Carry forward the four-part address Package.Level.Type.Instance read in three tiers: fixed leaves at level 1, fixed operators at level 2, and minted composites from level 3 whose level is their tree height.
status: inferred
conf: mid
src: 2.1.1#59
from: 3.1.2.1 3.1.2.2 1.2
why: The axioms file names the same four fields in the same order at line 59, (a-cell-is (package level type instance)); that the axiom's shape descends from the NUMS struct is inferred from the identical names.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8.2
name: node-is-category-plus-children
kind: finding
is: Carry forward the NUMS node shape: a category plus an ordered list of child IDs and nothing else, stored once per distinct shape. The use count kept per shape is a free signal of which shapes are hot.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.2.5 3.1.2.6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8.3
name: names-as-detachable-table
kind: finding
is: Carry forward names, and optionally literal values, as a detachable table of small ordinals beside a name-free structure. The rename experiment on NUMS shows that the node databases do not move when every identifier changes.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.3.1 3.1.3.2 3.1.3.3 1.7.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8.4
name: blueprint-side-shapes
kind: finding
is: Carry forward two blueprint-side shapes from NUMS: attributes such as public, embedded and parent sit on the reference to a blueprint, not on the blueprint; and a function is a pair of a structural signature blueprint and a body recipe.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.1.2.4 3.1.1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.8.5
name: cross-language-catalogue-with-effect
kind: finding
is: Carry forward a catalogue of leaves and operators drawn from many languages at once, with an effect split named in the category. NUMS declares TempWrite as a write with no global state change, but its Go handlers do not carry that: a plain assignment to a local is recorded as Write and a write through a pointer parameter as TempWrite.
status: inferred
conf: mid
src: 2.4.3#133-134 2.4.7#627-630 2.4.9#84-86
from: 3.1.2.3
limit: That the NUMS catalogue was meant as a cross-language operator alphabet is inferred from its non-Go entries and comments; only Go was ever read into it. Effect in the category is an intent to carry forward, not a property NUMS demonstrates.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.9
name: drop
kind: hub
is: Six things to drop from NUMS: IDs that are ordinals per module and per run; in-place update under a fixed ID; assertions that log and continue; declaring whatever does not resolve; Go spellings, hand-copied symbol numbers and a cgo parser in the core; ID spaces told apart only by a host type, map scans for names, and emit as the word for both ways.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.9.1
name: ordinal-per-run-ids
kind: finding
is: Let go of IDs that are ordinals per module and per run. Two parties that never met cannot compute the same number, and in NUMS the same input yields different numbers on different runs.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.2.6 3.1.3.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.1.9.4
name: auto-declare-unresolved
kind: finding
is: Let go of declaring whatever does not resolve. In NUMS that rule lets one file be read without its imports and turns a misspelled name into a new local, function, member or global, according to where it stands.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.1.3.6
at: 2026-11-24

